// ---- PART B: new author Sughra Humayun Mirza + her 2 confirmed works ----

CREATE (a:Author {name: "Sughra Humayun Mirza", bio: "Sughra Humayun Mirza (December 1884 – 1958), who also wrote under the pen name Haya, was an Urdu novelist, travel writer, and social reformer from Hyderabad, regarded as the city's first female novelist. Born to Safdar Ali Mirza, a surgeon in the Nizam's Army, and Maria Begum, a scholar of Arabic and Persian, she was homeschooled in Urdu and Persian due to the restrictions placed on the formal education of Muslim women at the time. At sixteen she married the barrister Syed Humayun Mirza, after whom Hyderabad's Humayun Nagar neighbourhood is named. Over her career she authored fourteen novels, including Zohra (1911), Sarguzisht-e-Hajira (1926), and Mohini (1929), alongside five travelogues documenting journeys within India and abroad — most notably Safarnama-e-Yurop (1926), an account of her travels through England, Germany, France, and Switzerland, during which she met the exiled Ottoman Caliph Abdulmejid II in Geneva. She edited the women's magazines Al-Nisa and Zaib-un-Nisa, and in 1919 founded the Anjuman-e-Khawateen-e-Dakkan (Deccan Ladies' Association), campaigning throughout her life for women's education, divorce rights, and widow remarriage. Her fiction and travel writing remain foundational to early Urdu women's literature from the Deccan.", language: "Urdu", id: 88});

// New Genre node needed — no existing "Travel account" genre in the DB
